// [imported] origin "data/standards/chemistry/H2Tart-formation.dat" (Choupo-2608 release) -- owned + sha256-verified by constant/propertyManifest
/*--------------------------------*- Choupo -*--------------------------------*\
  chemistry/H2Tart-formation.dat -- the neutral tartaric acid as the sum of
  its fully dissociated form:   Tart2- + 2 H+  <=>  H2Tart

  logK25 = pK1 + pK2 = 3.036 + 4.366 = 7.402 and dH = -(3.19 + 0.93) = -4.12 kJ/mol are the
  selected values of Goldberg, Kishore & Lennen, J. Phys. Chem. Ref. Data 31 (2002) 231,
  Table 7.63 (L-(+)-tartaric acid), p. 347 (pK 3.036 / 4.366, dH +3.19 / +0.93 kJ/mol at
  298.15 K and I = 0, evaluation grade ABA), the sign flipped for the direction written.  The
  independent measurement, Patterson & Woolley, J. Chem. Thermodyn. 33 (2001)
  1567-1585, Tables 9 and 10, agrees (pK 3.0360 / 4.366, dH 3.10 / 0.99 kJ/mol).

  CORRECTED 2026-11-12 (C372; the architect's authorisation).  Before: logK 7.32 (uncited
  handbook pK's 2.98 + 4.34) and dH -4.9 kJ/mol (the difference of two Kochergina,
  Volkov, Krutov & Krutova, Russ. J. Phys. Chem. 80 (2006) 899-903, Table 5 formation
  enthalpies).  The same Kochergina study still supplies the crystal datum in
  components/tartaricAcid.dat, the heat of solution in parameters/solution/
  tartaricAcid-water.dat and the species' hfAq; only the IONISATION enthalpy moved to
  Goldberg (-4.12 against -4.9, a 0.78 kJ/mol difference inside Kochergina's +/-1.5).

  This record and Tart-formation.dat are algebraic INVERSES of each other,
  written from the two different masters a case may declare; the closure
  activates whichever one its feed reaches and reports the other unreachable.
\*-----------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
recordType aqueousSpeciation;
schemaVersion 1;

species H2Tart; masters ( { ion Tart; nu 1; } { ion H; nu 2; } );
      logK25 7.402; dH -4120; source "Goldberg, Kishore & Lennen, J. Phys. Chem. Ref. Data 31 (2002) 231, Table 7.63, p. 347: pK1 3.036 + pK2 4.366 = 7.402; dH1 +3.19 + dH2 +0.93 = +4.12 kJ/mol, sign flipped (298.15 K, I = 0); agreeing measurement Patterson & Woolley, J. Chem. Thermodyn. 33 (2001) 1567, Tables 9 and 10";
